<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">King of the Universe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King of the Universe, or King of the World, was a prestigious Mesopotamian royal title signifying universal rule. First used by Akkadian kings, it was later adopted by Assyrian rulers to express claims to supremacy over all lands. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> it formed part of the ideology that justified expansion and conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-of-the-universe.jpg" alt="King of the Universe" width="297" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ALFGRN · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_of_Akkad_on_his_victory_stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Akkadian form of the title, šar kiššatim, derives from the Sumerian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>. In Sumerian legend Kish was where kingship was lowered from heaven after the flood, and the city was held to have a kind of primacy over the others. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic periods</a> rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> fought for standing, and the Mesopotamians believed their land to be the whole world, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, the lower sea, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the upper sea. Rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugal-zage-si">Lugal-zage-si</a> of Uruk already claimed to rule from sea to sea.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>, once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer">cupbearer</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-zababa">Ur-Zababa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, seized power there and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. He and his successors used the forms lugal ki-sár-ra and lugal kiš-ki without ruling Kish itself, and from their time the old title King of Kish should be understood as King of the Universe. Sargon's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> added King of the Four Corners of the World.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Earlier rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-uballit-i">Ashur-uballit I</a>, used the title from time to time, and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> claimed it against them. It was most prominent under the kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>, though from Sarduri I onwards the kings of Urartu took it too, to claim equality.</p><p>The title is not attested for every Neo-Assyrian king and sometimes appears only years into a reign, so it may have had to be earned; Stephanie Dalley proposed in 1998 that seven successful campaigns were needed. Of the Neo-Babylonian kings only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> used it in his own inscriptions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> adopted it after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE, and its last recorded use is on the Antiochus Cylinder of the Seleucid king Antiochus I Soter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Greek counterpart, kosmōkrator, was applied to gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a> and Pan, to the planets in astrology, and to rulers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus">Quintus Curtius Rufus</a> often calls <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> by it, and from the 2nd century CE it was used of Roman emperors. In Jewish writings king of the universe, melekh haolam, became a title of God, and traditional blessings begin with it; Christians use it for God, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> speaks of God as rabbi l-'alamin, Lord of the Universe. In Ephesians and later Christian writings kosmōkrator came to name evil spirits and the Devil.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King of the Universe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Territorial ambition first appeared under the Amorite conqueror Shamshi-Adad I (r. c. 1808–1776 BCE), who seized Assur, styled himself &quot;<b>King of the Universe</b>&quot; and built a kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia stretching from the Zagros foothills to the Euphrates and Mari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By 657 BC an Assyrian astrologer was calling the Cimmerian king Dugdammî šar-kiššati, '<b>King of the Universe</b>', a title normally held only by the king of Assyria and given to no other foreign ruler in the empire's history.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
